{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Software Design Patterns - Creational Patterns","content":"# Software Design Patterns - Creational Patterns\n\nThe creational patterns are: Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype, and Singleton.\n\n## Factory Method[ #](#factory-method)\n\nThe Factory Method pattern is **a way of declaring a method in the parent class and instantiating the object in a subclass**. In C++ it is usually expressed with virtual functions; in other languages such as Java, with abstract methods.\n\nThe Factory Method pattern focuses on the target \"product\" itself, and generally uses a common interface to instantiate an object's properties.\n\nTake a graphics-drawing application as an example. For the parent class we only care about the high-level requirement (drawing a shape); here that corresponds to a graphic. The structure is as follows:\n\n![Factory Method - Graphics](/post/softwaredesignpattern2/7f1ca2472da5.png)\n\nThe definition looks like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Graphics {\n    // Abstract base class\nprotected:\n    virtual void paint() = 0;\n};\n```\n\nThe parent class (the graphics class) is an abstract high-level class. To draw different concrete shapes, we can define:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Triangle : public Graphics {\n    // Class that draws a triangle\nprotected:\n    void paint() override {\n        // TODO: draw triangle\n    }\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Graphics {\n    // Class that draws a circle\nprotected:\n    void paint() override {\n        // TODO: draw circle\n    }\n};\n```\n\nNow, to draw a circle, we just declare a `Circle` object and call the common `paint()` to draw it. Every other shape works the same way. This greatly reduces the cost of understanding: we only need to focus on the \"product\" (the circle, `Circle`).\n\n## Abstract Factory[ #](#abstract-factory)\n\nAbstract Factory is **a way to create objects without specifying the concrete class to instantiate**. It focuses on the different \"styles\" of constructing objects, and one factory typically constructs more than one kind of \"product\".\n\nContinuing with the drawing example above, suppose we now want to draw shapes in special styles: some with a brush, some with a pencil, some with pastels, or with a blender. The structure is as follows:\n\n![Abstract Factory - Graphics](/post/softwaredesignpattern2/61bbb9c0a974.png)\n\nFour drawing factories are defined here, one per style. Each factory uses a common interface to draw different shapes (it can construct three different \"products\").\n\nThe code is defined as follows:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass GraphicsFactory {\n    // Base class\nprotected:\n    virtual void paintTriangle() = 0;\n    virtual void paintCircle() = 0;\n    virtual void paintSquare() = 0;\n};\n\nclass BrushFactory {\n    // Brush drawing factory\nprotected:\n    void paintTriangle() override {\n        // TODO: draw triangle\n    }\n    void paintCircle() override {\n        // TODO: draw circle\n    }\n    void paintSquare() override {\n        // TODO: draw square\n    }\n};\n\nclass PencilFactory {\n    // Pencil drawing factory\nprotected:\n    void paintTriangle() override {\n        // TODO: draw triangle\n    }\n    void paintCircle() override {\n        // TODO: draw circle\n    }\n    void paintSquare() override {\n        // TODO: draw square\n    }\n};\n\nclass PastelFactory {\n    // Pastel drawing factory\nprotected:\n    void paintTriangle() override {\n        // TODO: draw triangle\n    }\n    void paintCircle() override {\n        // TODO: draw circle\n    }\n    void paintSquare() override {\n        // TODO: draw square\n    }\n};\n\nclass BlendFactory {\n    // Blender drawing factory\nprotected:\n    void paintTriangle() override {\n        // TODO: draw triangle\n    }\n    void paintCircle() override {\n        // TODO: draw circle\n    }\n    void paintSquare() override {\n        // TODO: draw square\n    }\n};\n```\n\nNow, to draw a triangle in pencil style, we no longer need to care about a triangle class; the same goes for the others.\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nPencilFactory pFactory;\npFactory.paintTriangle(); // pencil draws triangle\npFactory.paintCircle(); // pencil draws circle\nBrushFactory bFactory;\nbFactory.paintCircle(); // brush draws circle\nbFactory.paintSquare(); // brush draws square\n```\n\n## Builder[ #](#builder)\n\nThe Builder pattern is **a way to create complex objects step by step**. It lets us flexibly customize how each part of an object is created.\n\nWith a builder, you call various interfaces one after another to perform specific initialization and set the object's properties. Once initialization is done, you call a build function (`build()`) to produce the target object.\n\nUsing drawing as the example:\n\n![Builder - Graphics](/post/softwaredesignpattern2/cd999fa23714.png)\n\nThree drawing styles are defined here. With the builder, you only initialize the shapes in the styles you want, then call the build interface (`paint()`) to draw the target picture.\n\nThe code is as follows:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Builder {\n    Builder& triangleBrush() {\n        // TODO: add brush-drawn triangle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& circleBrush() {\n        // TODO: add brush-drawn circle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& squareBrush() {\n        // TODO: add brush-drawn square\n        return *this;\n    }\n\n    Builder& trianglePencil() {\n        // TODO: add pencil-drawn triangle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& circlePencil() {\n        // TODO: add pencil-drawn circle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& squarePencil() {\n        // TODO: add pencil-drawn square\n        return *this;\n    }\n\n    Builder& trianglePastel() {\n        // TODO: add pastel-drawn triangle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& circlePastel() {\n        // TODO: add pastel-drawn circle\n        return *this;\n    }\n    Builder& squarePastel() {\n        // TODO: add pastel-drawn square\n        return *this;\n    }\n\n    void paint() {\n        // TODO: build/render function\n    }\n};\n```\n\nTo draw a complex picture with the Builder pattern, you can do this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nBuilder builder;\nbuilder.circlePastel() // initialize circle\n       .squareBrush() // initialize square\n       .trianglePencil() // initialize triangle\n       .paint() // build\n```\n\n## Prototype[ #](#prototype)\n\nNormally, objects are obtained from a class definition. But if we don't know the class and just want to **clone a new object from an existing object**, carrying over some of its properties, that approach is the Prototype pattern.\n\nNote that cloning hides the complex details of the class. A clone can copy public methods and fields as well as protected or private ones, so that it reproduces the original object's properties as fully as possible.\n\n![Prototype - Graphics](/post/softwaredesignpattern2/3716ab58195a.png)\n\nExample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass ShapeGraphics {\n    // Shape\npublic:\n    ShapeGraphics() : mX(0), mY(0), mColor(0) {}\n    ShapeGraphics(int x, int y, int cr)\n        : mX(x), mY(y), mColor(cr)\n    {}\n    ShapeGraphics(const ShapeGraphics& shape) {\n        this->mX = shape.mX;\n        this->mY = shape.mY;\n        this->mColor = shape.mColor;\n    }\n    virtual ShapeGraphics* clone() = 0;\nprivate:\n    int mX, mY, mColor;\n};\n\nclass CircleGraphics : public ShapeGraphics {\n    // Circle\npublic:\n    CircleGraphics(const CircleGraphics& source)\n        : ShapeGraphics(source)\n    {\n        this.mRadius = source.mRadius;\n    }\n    ShapeGraphics* clone() {\n        return new CircleGraphics(*this);\n    }\nprivate:\n    int mRadius;\n};\n\nclass TriangleGraphics : public ShapeGraphics {\n    // Triangle\npublic:\n    TriangleGraphics(const TriangleGraphics& source)\n        : ShapeGraphics(source)\n    {\n        this->mA = source.mA;\n        this->mB = source.mB;\n        this->mC = source.mC;\n    }\n    ShapeGraphics* clone() {\n        return new TriangleGraphics(*this);\n    }\nprivate:\n    int mA, mB, mC;\n};\n\nclass SquareGraphics : public ShapeGraphics {\n    // Square\npublic:\n    SquareGraphics(const SquareGraphics& source)\n        : ShapeGraphics(source)\n    {\n        this->mWidth = source.mWidth;\n        this->mHeight = source.mHeight;\n    }\n    ShapeGraphics* clone() {\n        return new SquareGraphics(*this);\n    }\nprivate:\n    int mWidth, mHeight;\n};\n```\n\nConstructing objects with the Prototype pattern looks like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass CustomShape : public ShapeGraphics {\n    CustomShape() : ShapeGraphics(10, 10, 0xff00) {}\n    ShapeGraphics* clone() {\n        return new CustomShape(*this);\n    }\n};\n\nCustomShape shape; // position (10, 10), color 0xff00\nauto newshape = shape.clone(); // clone the shape\nSquareGraphics square(*newshape); // construct an object from the cloned newshape attributes\nauto newsquare = square.clone(); // new cloned square with square attributes\n```\n\n## Singleton[ #](#singleton)\n\nA singleton is a special construction pattern: the object it constructs has only one global instance at runtime.\n\nSingletons are typically used for central control and centralized configuration, ensuring consistency of information and of control.\n\nExample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Singleton {\n    // Singleton\npublic:\n    static Singleton& getInstance() {\n        // declare a function-local static instance\n        static Singleton inst;\n        return inst;\n    }\nprivate:\n    Singleton() {}\n};\n```\n\nThis defines a singleton class. It cannot be instantiated through the class directly; instead, a static variable and a static function provide access to the object.\n\nUsage:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nauto inst = Singleton::getInstance(); // get the singleton instance\n```\n","locale":"en","slug":"softwaredesignpattern2"}}